<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lower Navarre</h1><p class="st-meta">1530 CE – 1620 CE · capital Saint-Palais</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The fragment of the Kingdom of Navarre north of the Pyrenees, ruled by the Albret kings and the future Henry IV until its union with France in 1620.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ferdinand of Aragon conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> in 1512. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a> kings kept a small Basque-speaking territory on the northern side around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-jean-pied-de-port">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-palais-pyrenees-atlantiques">Saint-Palais</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> gave up around 1530 as too hard to hold.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a> monarchs, who also ruled neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Béarn</a>, kept the royal title and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre's</a> own estates and courts, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-palais-pyrenees-atlantiques">Saint-Palais</a> as the seat of government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Queen Jeanne d'Albret</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> and promoted it, though most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> stayed Catholic. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry III of Navarre</a> became Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1589.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> issued an edict uniting Lower Navarre and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Béarn</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>, and French kings were styled 'King of France and Navarre' until 1830.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lower-navarre.jpg" alt="Lower Navarre" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baja_Navarra-Pirineos_Atl%C3%A1nticos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1530 CE – 1620 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Palais</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry II of Navarre, Jeanne d'Albret, Henry IV of France</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity and Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Basque</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Navarre (north of the Pyrenees), Basse-Navarre</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=lower-navarre" aria-label="Lower Navarre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4650 646 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lower Navarre lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4970 902L4960 913M4970 902L4991 897M4970 902L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4960 913h0M4991 897h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4970 902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=lower-navarre">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
